    eyebrow: FAQ
    heading: Questions people
    heading_accent: ask us most
    heading_suffix: ''
    items:
      - question: How can I raise an issue to ONDC?
        answer: You can raise your complaint on the <a
          href="https://portal.ondc.org/complaints" target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">ONDC Grievance Portal</a>. Please ensure that you
          provide the ticket number issued by the Buyer App along with the
          complaint. Alternatively, you can file a ticket through the National
          Consumer Helpline. Please make sure you include the name of the Buyer
          App in your complaint.
      - question: Where can I find the issue Ticket Number?
        answer: When you file a complaint with the Buyer App, they will issue a ticket
          number (sometimes called complaint number) to you. That number has to
          be quoted in your complaint to ONDC. All Buyer Apps are mandated to
          issue ticket numbers for complaints according to the ONDC Network
          Policy.
      - question: Can I file a complaint directly with ONDC without first raising it
          with the respective Buyer App?
        answer: Since you placed your order through the Buyer App, you must first raise
          the complaint with the Buyer App, and give it (and other parties
          involved) an opportunity to investigate and resolve the issue. The
          ONDC Network Policy mandates the Buyer App to convey clear resolution
          timelines once you’ve filed a complaint. If you don’t receive
          resolution within the promised timeline, the issue can be raised to
          ONDC for closure.
      - question: The Buyer App has responded to my grievance and provided me with a
          resolution, but I am not satisfied with the resolution? What should I
          do next?
        answer: You can raise the complaint to ONDC through the <a
          href="https://portal.ondc.org/complaints" target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">ONDC Grievance Portal</a>. ONDC will then work with the
          Buyer App, Seller App and seller to get your complaint resolved. If
          you’re still unhappy after this, you may approach any of the
          empanelled ODR service providers listed on the ONDC website, or the
          consumer courts.
      - question: The promised timeline for the Buyer App to resolve the complaint is
          too high. Can my resolution be faster?
        answer: All Network Participants must resolve complaints within the timelines
          specified by applicable law. Under the Consumer Protection
          (E-commerce) Rules 2020, the maximum time to respond is 48 hours, and
          to resolve is 30 days. If the Buyer App has given a longer timeline
          than the law allows, you can file a complaint with ONDC, which will
          work with the Buyer App to bring it within the legal limits.
      - question: I am not satisfied with the Resolution provided by the Buyer app and
          will approach the Consumer court to provide me with a better
          resolution. Can ONDC help?
        answer: The grievance process ONDC provides does not diminish your right to seek
          relief through the courts or alternative dispute resolution (mediation
          or arbitration). ONDC also lists empanelled Online Dispute Resolution
          service providers if you’d prefer mediation, conciliation or
          arbitration over going to court.
